/* ======================================================================
   The six philosophy axes. PHIL_EFFECTS[i][j] = what answer j to statement i does (from its lean).
   E: Duty (-) to Outcomes (+)   K: Faith and gut (-) to Evidence (+)   M: Spiritual mind (-) to Physical mind (+)
   V: Objective values (-) to Relative values (+)   C: Confident (-) to Sceptical (+)   W: Free will (-) to Determinism (+)
   ====================================================================== */
const PHIL_AXES = {
  E: ['Duty', 'Outcomes'], K: ['Faith and gut', 'Evidence'], M: ['Spiritual mind', 'Physical mind'],
  V: ['Objective values', 'Relative values'], C: ['Confident', 'Sceptical'], W: ['Free will', 'Determinism'],
};
const PHIL_EFFECTS = PHIL_QUESTIONS.map(p => POSITION_VALUE.map(v => {
  const e = {};
  Object.keys(p.lean).forEach(k => { if(v) e[k] = p.lean[k] * v; });
  return e;
}));
// Ideal profiles (0 to 100 on each axis) for the six philosophy types; you get the nearest one.
const PHIL_TYPES = {
  Empiricist:    { E:50, K:90, M:80, V:50, C:60, W:60 },
  Rationalist:   { E:50, K:85, M:60, V:15, C:20, W:50 },
  Utilitarian:   { E:90, K:70, M:70, V:50, C:40, W:60 },
  Stoic:         { E:25, K:60, M:50, V:25, C:40, W:70 },
  Existentialist:{ E:40, K:40, M:40, V:85, C:60, W:20 },
  Skeptic:       { E:50, K:50, M:60, V:70, C:90, W:50 },
};

/* Questions reworded since some tests were started: old wording -> [new wording, {old answer: new answer}].
   A saved test is translated when it is resumed, so its answers still count. */
const QUESTION_RENAMES = {
  "The trolley problem (Philippa Foot): a runaway trolley will kill five people unless you divert it onto a side track, where it will kill one. Should you divert it?":["It would be right to divert a runaway train onto a track where it will kill one person, if that saves five others.",{"Yes. Consequentialism: the right act is the one with the best outcome, and five lives outweigh one":"Strongly agree","Yes, but it leaves moral residue: diverting is right, yet killing the one is still a wrong to regret (Bernard Williams)":"Agree","No. The doctrine of doing and allowing: actively killing one is worse than letting five die":"Disagree","There is no fact of the matter. Emotivism: calling an act right only expresses our feelings about it (A.J. Ayer)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Kant's categorical imperative: act only on a rule you could will everyone to follow. Is that the right test of a moral action?":["Some moral rules must never be broken, whatever the consequences.",{"Yes. Morality is a matter of duties that bind us whatever the consequences (Kant)":"Strongly agree","Largely. There are real duties, but they can conflict, and judgement decides which wins (W.D. Ross)":"Agree","Only as a rule of thumb. Rules matter because following them generally has good results (rule consequentialism)":"Disagree","No. Only consequences matter, and any rule should be broken when breaking it does more good (act utilitarianism)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Plato's Ring of Gyges: a ring makes you invisible, so you could do wrong and never be caught. Would you still have a reason to be just?":["If you could do wrong and never be caught, there would be no good reason left to act fairly.",{"Yes. Justice is good in itself, whatever it brings, and the unjust soul is harmed by its injustice (Plato)":"Strongly disagree","Yes. Being just is part of being a good, flourishing person, so injustice would damage my life (virtue ethics)":"Disagree","Only for what it brings. Justice is an agreement we keep because everyone gains from cooperation (Hobbes)":"Agree","No. Justice is a convention that protects the weak, and with the ring it would be rational to ignore it (Glaucon, Thrasymachus)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "W.K. Clifford said it is wrong to believe anything on insufficient evidence. William James replied that some beliefs may rightly go beyond the evidence. Who is right?":["It is wrong to believe anything without enough evidence for it.",{"Clifford. It is wrong always, everywhere and for anyone to believe on insufficient evidence":"Strongly agree","Mostly Clifford, though we may act on probable beliefs when a decision can't wait":"Agree","James. Where evidence can't settle a question and the choice matters, we may believe by an act of will":"Disagree","Neither goes far enough. Some of the deepest truths are known by the heart, not by evidence (Pascal)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Derek Parfit's teleporter: a machine scans you, destroys your body, and builds an exact copy on Mars with all your memories. Is the person on Mars you?":["If a machine destroyed your body and built an exact copy with all your memories, the copy would really be you.",{"Yes. Personal identity is psychological continuity: same memories and personality, same person (Locke)":"Strongly agree","Identity isn't what matters. What matters is that my psychology carries on, and it does (Parfit)":"Agree","No. I would die, and a new person would be made. A self is more than the information it contains":"Disagree","The question has no determinate answer. Our idea of 'the same person' breaks down in cases like this":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Descartes' evil demon: you cannot rule out that a powerful demon is deceiving you about everything you perceive. Can we know anything about the outside world?":["We can never be certain that the world outside our own minds exists.",{"Yes, certainly. I know I have hands better than I could ever know a sceptical premise (G.E. Moore)":"Strongly disagree","Yes. Knowledge needs reliable, well-justified belief, not certainty (fallibilism)":"Disagree","Only in a weak sense. The sceptic can't be refuted, so our knowledge rests on assumptions we can't prove":"Agree","No. Radical scepticism is right, and we should suspend judgement about the outside world (Pyrrho, Sextus Empiricus)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "The central question of metaethics: are there objective moral truths, true whatever anyone believes?":["Some actions are morally wrong whatever any person or culture believes about them.",{"Yes. Moral realism: some moral claims, such as 'torturing for fun is wrong', are objectively true":"Strongly agree","Yes, grounded in human nature: moral truths are facts about what makes humans flourish (Aristotle, Philippa Foot)":"Agree","Only relative ones. Moral truths exist, but only relative to a culture or an agreement (relativism, constructivism)":"Disagree","No. Error theory: there are no moral facts, so every moral claim is false (J.L. Mackie)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The problem of free will: if every event, including every choice, is caused by earlier events, can people be morally responsible for what they do?":["Our choices are not fully decided by earlier causes; we could genuinely have chosen differently.",{"Yes, because our choices are not fully caused. Libertarian free will: we could have chosen otherwise (Kant, Thomas Reid)":"Strongly agree","Yes. Compatibilism: you are free when you act on your own reasons, even if those reasons were caused (Hume, Frankfurt)":"Agree","Only partly. Determinism means no one truly deserves blame, though praise and blame are still useful":"Disagree","No. Hard determinism: no one could have done otherwise, so no one truly deserves blame (Spinoza, Pereboom)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Are intuitions evidence? When something just seems true to us, as many moral and philosophical claims do, does that count as a reason to believe it?":["If something seems clearly true when we think it over carefully, that is good evidence it is true, even without proof.",{"No. Intuitions are products of bias and upbringing; only empirical evidence counts":"Strongly disagree","A little. They are weak evidence, to be checked against theory and data":"Disagree","We can't tell. Intuitions differ across people and cultures, so we should suspend judgement":"Agree","Yes. Some truths in ethics and mathematics are known directly by intuition (intuitionism, G.E. Moore)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Cultural relativism: anthropologists find very different moral codes in different cultures. Does this show there is no universal morality?":["An action can be wrong in one culture and right in another, without either culture being mistaken.",{"No. Disagreement doesn't mean there's no truth, and reason can settle who is right (Kant)":"Strongly disagree","No. Beneath the differences lie shared human values; cultures mostly differ in circumstances (James Rachels)":"Disagree","Partly. A few core values may be universal, but much of morality is genuinely relative":"Agree","Yes. Right and wrong are whatever a culture's code says ('custom is king of all', Herodotus)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Can the mind survive the death of the body? (Plato's Phaedo against the materialists)":["The mind can go on existing after the body dies.",{"Yes. The soul is a non-physical substance that can exist without the body (Plato, Descartes)":"Strongly agree","Possibly. If the mind is more than the brain, something of the person may continue (Aquinas)":"Agree","No. The mind is what the brain does, so it ends when the brain does (physicalism, Epicurus)":"Disagree","We cannot know. Questions like this lie beyond the limits of possible knowledge (Kant)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Philosophy of religion: does belief in God need argument or evidence to be rational?":["Believing in God can be reasonable even without any argument or evidence for it.",{"No. Faith is its own way of knowing and needs no outside support (fideism, Kierkegaard)":"Strongly agree","No. Belief in God can be 'properly basic', rational without argument, like belief in other minds (Alvin Plantinga)":"Agree","Yes, and there are good arguments for it (natural theology, Aquinas' Five Ways)":"Disagree","Yes, and without strong evidence we should not believe it (evidentialism, Bertrand Russell)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The problem of taste (Hume, Kant): is beauty a real feature of things, or only in the eye of the beholder?":["Some things are more beautiful than others, regardless of anyone's personal taste.",{"Real. Beauty is a property of things, which trained judgement can detect (Plato, Aquinas)":"Strongly agree","In between. Judgements of beauty rest on feeling, yet rightly claim everyone's agreement (Kant, Hume's standard of taste)":"Agree","Mostly cultural. Standards of beauty come from the traditions we are raised in":"Disagree","In the eye of the beholder. Beauty is purely personal taste":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Philosophy of history: does human history show real progress?":["Over the centuries, humanity has made real moral progress.",{"Yes. Reason and science drive steady moral and material progress (Condorcet, Hegel)":"Strongly agree","Mostly, though progress is fragile and can be reversed (Kant's cautious hope)":"Agree","Only in our tools. Technology advances, but human nature does not (Schopenhauer)":"Disagree","No. History has no direction, and every gain brings a new loss (Rousseau, cyclical views of history)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Philosophy of punishment: what justifies punishing someone who has broken the law?":["Criminals should be punished because they deserve it, even if the punishment prevents no future crime.",{"Retribution: wrongdoers deserve to suffer in proportion to their crime (Kant)":"Strongly agree","Mostly what the wrongdoer deserves, though mercy and remorse can rightly reduce it (mixed theories, H.L.A. Hart)":"Agree","Deterrence and protection: punishment is justified only by the harm it prevents (Bentham)":"Disagree","Nothing but reform: blame is pointless, so punishment should only rehabilitate":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The mind-body problem: what is the relationship between the mind and the brain?":["The mind is nothing more than the physical activity of the brain.",{"Physicalism: the mind is nothing but brain activity (identity theory)":"Strongly agree","Functionalism: the mind is what the brain does, and can't be reduced to its chemistry, though it is still physical":"Agree","Property dualism: the brain is physical, but conscious experience is a non-physical property it gives rise to (Chalmers)":"Disagree","Substance dualism: the mind is a non-physical soul that interacts with the body (Descartes)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Philosophy of religion: does God exist?":["There is a God who created the universe.",{"Theism: a personal God exists, who created the world and cares for it":"Strongly agree","Deism or pantheism: something divine exists, but not a personal God who intervenes (Spinoza)":"Agree","Agnosticism: we cannot know whether God exists, so we should suspend judgement":"Disagree","Atheism: there is no God":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Rationalism or empiricism: where does our knowledge ultimately come from?":["Everything we know about the world ultimately comes from experience and observation.",{"Empiricism: all knowledge comes from experience and observation (Locke, Hume)":"Strongly agree","Kant's synthesis: experience supplies the content of knowledge, and reason supplies its structure":"Agree","Rationalism: the most important truths are known by reason and intuition alone (Plato, Descartes)":"Disagree","Revelation: some truths can only be known through faith or divine revelation (Augustine)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Normative ethics: which theory comes closest to explaining what makes an action right?":["Whether an action is right or wrong depends only on its consequences.",{"Deontology: right actions follow moral duties and rules, whatever the consequences (Kant)":"Strongly disagree","Virtue ethics: right actions are what a person of good character would do (Aristotle)":"Disagree","Consequentialism: right actions produce the best overall outcomes (Bentham, Mill)":"Agree","Contractualism: right actions follow principles no one could reasonably reject (Scanlon)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Personal identity: what makes you the same person over a lifetime?":["Each of us has a soul or inner self that stays the same throughout our life.",{"Being the same living organism, with the same body and brain (animalism)":"Strongly disagree","Continuity of memory and personality (the psychological view, Locke)":"Disagree","An immaterial soul that persists through every change (Descartes, Swinburne)":"Agree","Nothing. There is no lasting self, only a stream of experiences (Hume's bundle theory, the Buddha)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Scientific realism: do our best scientific theories describe reality as it really is?":["Our best scientific theories describe the world as it really is, including the parts we cannot observe.",{"Yes. The success of science would be a miracle if its theories weren't approximately true (Hilary Putnam)":"Strongly agree","In outline. Science gets the structure of reality right, even when the details change (structural realism)":"Agree","We don't need to say. Theories are tools for predicting what we observe, not pictures of hidden reality (instrumentalism, van Fraassen)":"Disagree","No. Science is shaped by its time and culture, one paradigm among others (Kuhn, Feyerabend)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Kant's murderer at the door: a murderer asks where your friend is hiding. Kant said you still must not lie. Was he right?":["Lying is wrong even when a lie would save someone's life.",{"Yes. Lying is always wrong, and you answer for your duty, not for the murderer's choices":"Strongly agree","Partly. You may refuse to answer or stay silent, but lying itself remains wrong":"Agree","No. Saving a life clearly outweighs the duty of honesty; the consequences decide":"Disagree","No, and the case shows moral rules are only conventions, to be dropped when they don't serve us":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The transplant case (Judith Jarvis Thomson): a surgeon could secretly kill one healthy patient and use his organs to save five. Should she?":["It would be right for a surgeon to secretly sacrifice one patient to save five others with his organs.",{"Never. It uses a person merely as a means, violating his dignity (Kant's formula of humanity)":"Strongly disagree","No, though the numbers matter. You may not intend harm as a means, even for a good end (double effect)":"Disagree","Yes, in principle. Five lives outweigh one, and our horror at it is a bias (strict utilitarianism)":"Agree","There is no fact of the matter. Our clashing intuitions show ethics is a matter of feeling":"Strongly agree"}],
  "The deathbed promise: you promised a dying friend to give their money to one charity, but another would do far more good. What should you do?":["A promise should be kept even when breaking it would do more good.",{"Keep it. A promise creates a duty that holds even when breaking it would do more good (W.D. Ross)":"Strongly agree","Keep it, with regret. The duty of fidelity outweighs the gain, but the lost good still counts":"Agree","Break it. Act utilitarianism: do whatever produces the most good":"Disagree","Keep to its purpose, not its letter: give where your friend would have wanted, had they known the facts":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Nozick's experience machine: a machine could give you a lifetime of perfect experiences that feel completely real. Would you plug in for good?":["A life of perfect happiness inside a simulation would be just as good as a real life.",{"Yes. Hedonism: pleasure is the only thing good in itself, and the machine delivers it (Bentham)":"Strongly agree","Probably. If it feels identical, it's hard to say what would be missing":"Agree","No. We want to actually do things and be a certain kind of person, not just have experiences (Nozick)":"Disagree","No. Truth, real relationships and achievement are good in themselves (objective list theory)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Searle's Chinese Room: could a computer that perfectly imitates human conversation genuinely understand and feel, or only simulate it?":["A computer could one day have real thoughts and feelings, not just imitate them.",{"Genuinely. Functionalism: a mind is what a system does, not what it's made of":"Strongly agree","Probably, if it reproduced the brain's causal workings closely enough":"Agree","Only simulate. Running a program is shuffling symbols, which is not understanding (John Searle)":"Disagree","Only simulate. Real consciousness needs a soul or something beyond matter that no machine can have":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The hard problem of consciousness (David Chalmers): why does brain activity feel like anything from the inside, like the redness of red?":["Conscious experience, such as what it is like to see red, cannot be fully explained by facts about the brain.",{"It isn't a real problem. Once the brain's functions are explained, nothing is left over (Daniel Dennett)":"Strongly disagree","It is hard, but neuroscience will eventually solve it in physical terms":"Disagree","It shows conscious experience is something extra, beyond the physical facts (Chalmers, Frank Jackson's Mary)":"Agree","It shows consciousness is fundamental, more basic than matter itself (idealism, panpsychism)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "The Ship of Theseus: every plank of a ship is replaced, one by one, until none of the original is left. Is it still the same ship?":["Some questions about identity, such as whether a fully rebuilt ship is still the same ship, have no true answer.",{"Yes. Identity follows continuity of form and function over time":"Strongly disagree","It depends on our purposes. 'Same ship' is a matter of convention, not fact":"Disagree","No. Identity follows the original matter, so it is a new ship":"Agree","There is no fact of the matter. The puzzle shows our concept of identity is vague":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Nick Bostrom's simulation argument: if advanced civilisations run vast numbers of simulated minds, most minds like ours would be simulated. Are we probably living in a simulation?":["It is quite possible that we are living in a computer simulation.",{"No. It is untestable speculation, so there is no reason to take it seriously":"Strongly disagree","Unlikely. Its premises are doubtful and there is no evidence for it":"Disagree","Possibly. The argument is hard to refute, so I can't rule it out":"Agree","Probably. If its premises hold, the odds favour it":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Benjamin Libet's experiments: the brain starts preparing a movement before people feel they have decided to move. What does this show about free will?":["Free will is an illusion: the brain decides before we are aware of choosing.",{"Little. The experiments measured trivial urges, not real decisions, and we can still veto them":"Strongly disagree","That choices have unconscious roots, but freedom as compatibilists understand it survives":"Disagree","That most of our decisions are made before we become aware of them":"Agree","That free will is an illusion: the feeling of choosing comes after the brain has decided (Daniel Wegner)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Moral luck (Thomas Nagel): two equally reckless drunk drivers; one kills a child by chance, the other gets home safely. Is the first more to blame?":["A drunk driver who kills someone deserves more blame than an equally drunk driver who happened to get home safely.",{"Yes. What actually happens matters morally, not just what we intended":"Strongly agree","Somewhat. Outcomes carry some moral weight, though the recklessness is the core wrong":"Agree","No. Only what is in our control can be judged, so they are equally to blame (Kant)":"Disagree","The case shows blame itself is confused: neither controlled what made them who they are":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Karl Popper's paradox of tolerance: should a tolerant society tolerate movements that would destroy tolerance?":["Free speech should protect even people who want to abolish free speech.",{"Yes. Freedom of speech must apply to everyone, as a matter of principle":"Strongly agree","Mostly, until they pose a real and present danger (John Rawls)":"Agree","No. Unlimited tolerance leads to the end of tolerance, so a free society may suppress them (Popper)":"Disagree","There is no neutral answer. What counts as tolerance is set by whoever holds power (Herbert Marcuse)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The moral status of animals: is it wrong to make animals suffer for human benefit, such as food or medical research?":["Making animals suffer is wrong even when it brings big benefits to humans.",{"Yes. Animals have rights, and using them merely as means is wrong whatever the gains (Tom Regan)":"Strongly agree","Their suffering counts as much as ours, so it is wrong unless the benefit is truly huge (Peter Singer)":"Agree","Their suffering counts, but human benefits can outweigh it if it is kept to a minimum (animal welfarism)":"Disagree","Animals lack moral standing of their own; we only have duties about them, not to them (Kant)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Moral progress: when past societies accepted slavery, were they mistaken about a moral truth?":["Slavery was wrong even in times when almost everyone accepted it.",{"Yes. Moral truths are timeless, and they were simply wrong":"Strongly agree","Yes, and moral progress is the slow discovery of more of the truth":"Agree","Only by our standards. Judged by the standards of their own time, they were not":"Disagree","The question has no answer. Morality is whatever a society accepts":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Philosophy of mathematics: do numbers exist, and is mathematics discovered or invented?":["Mathematical truths, such as 2 + 2 = 4, would be true even if no one had ever existed.",{"Platonism: numbers are real abstract objects, existing independently of us, and we discover mathematics":"Strongly agree","Mathematics describes real structures in the world, which reason discovers (structuralism, Aristotle)":"Agree","Formalism: mathematics is a game of symbols and rules that happens to be useful":"Disagree","Constructivism: mathematics is a human invention, shaped by our language and culture":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Occam's razor: do not multiply entities beyond necessity. When two theories fit the evidence equally well, should we believe the simpler one?":["When two explanations fit the facts equally well, the simpler one is more likely to be true.",{"Yes, always. Simplicity is a mark of truth":"Strongly agree","Usually, as a sound rule of method, though not a guarantee":"Agree","No. Reality may be complicated; fit with everything else we know matters more":"Disagree","Neither, until further evidence decides between them":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Hume on miracles: a wise person proportions belief to the evidence, so testimony to a miracle should almost never be believed. Is he right?":["Reports of miracles should almost never be believed.",{"Yes. It is always more likely the witness is mistaken than that a law of nature was broken":"Strongly agree","Mostly, though strong enough testimony could in principle establish a miracle":"Agree","No. Honest, sober witnesses deserve belief even about the extraordinary (Thomas Reid's principle of credulity)":"Disagree","No. Miracles are possible, and refusing all testimony to them is dogmatic naturalism":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Metaphysical naturalism: is the natural world, the world science studies, all there is?":["Nothing exists beyond the natural world that science studies.",{"Yes. Everything that exists is part of nature and open to science":"Strongly agree","Probably, though science is unfinished, so I can't be certain":"Agree","Probably not. Some realities, such as minds or values, may lie beyond science":"Disagree","No. There is a spiritual or supernatural reality beyond nature":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Leibniz asked: why is there something rather than nothing?":["The universe exists because something beyond it, such as God, brought it into being.",{"Because a necessary being, God, is the cause of everything that exists (the cosmological argument)":"Strongly agree","There is a deeper reason in reality, even if we can't grasp it (the principle of sufficient reason)":"Agree","There is no reason. The universe is simply a brute fact (Bertrand Russell)":"Disagree","The question may be meaningless, since it asks for a cause outside all causes (Wittgenstein)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The meaning of life: does human life have meaning, and where would it come from?":["Life has a meaning that exists whether or not we choose to give it one.",{"Yes, given by God or by the purpose built into the universe":"Strongly agree","Yes, objectively, in things truly worth doing, such as love, knowledge and excellence (Aristotle, Susan Wolf)":"Agree","Not in itself, but we create meaning through our own choices (existentialism, Sartre)":"Disagree","No. Life is meaningless, and the search for meaning is in vain (nihilism)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Epicurus argued that death is nothing to us: where death is, we are not. Is death bad for the person who dies?":["Death is simply the end of a person; nothing of us carries on.",{"Not if there is an afterlife. Death is a passage, not an end":"Strongly disagree","Yes. Death deprives us of all the good things we would have had (the deprivation account, Thomas Nagel)":"Disagree","No. Epicurus is right: once dead, there is no one left to be harmed":"Agree","No, and fearing it is irrational: it is just the end of a physical process (Lucretius)":"Strongly agree"}],
  "John Rawls' veil of ignorance: you choose the rules for a society without knowing what place you will have in it. Which principle would you choose?":["A just society should put the worst-off first, even if that means less wealth or happiness overall.",{"Equal basic liberties for everyone, which no gain in welfare can override (Rawls' first principle)":"Strongly agree","Equal liberties first, then allow inequalities only if they benefit the worst off (the difference principle)":"Agree","Whatever maximises average happiness, betting on the odds (John Harsanyi's utilitarianism)":"Disagree","No abstract principle; rely on what has actually worked in real societies (Burke, Hayek)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Chesterton's fence: don't take down a fence until you know why it was put up. How much authority should tradition have?":["Long-standing traditions deserve respect even when we can't see what they are for.",{"A great deal. Traditions carry the wisdom of generations, which reason alone can't replace (Edmund Burke)":"Strongly agree","Real weight. There is probably a reason we can't see, so change should be cautious (Michael Oakeshott)":"Agree","Some, but a tradition has to justify itself by its results":"Disagree","None in itself. Only reason and evidence count, and age proves nothing (Bentham, Thomas Paine)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Sartre said we are 'condemned to be free': whatever our circumstances, we are wholly responsible for what we make of ourselves. Is he right?":["Whatever our circumstances, each of us is fully responsible for what we make of our life.",{"Yes. Circumstances limit our options, but they never choose for us (existentialism)":"Strongly agree","Largely. Circumstances matter, but we remain the authors of our choices":"Agree","Largely not. Upbringing, class and genes shape most of what we do (Marx, social determinism)":"Disagree","No. Our choices are entirely the product of causes we didn't choose (hard determinism)":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "When an argument that seems valid, from premises that seem true, leads to a conclusion that seems plainly false, what should we do?":["If an argument seems flawless but leads to a conclusion that seems absurd, we should accept the conclusion.",{"Accept the conclusion. If the premises are true and the logic valid, intuition must give way":"Strongly agree","Look for the faulty premise, but accept the conclusion if none can be found":"Agree","Reject a premise. We can be more certain the conclusion is false than that the premises are true (G.E. Moore)":"Disagree","Distrust clever arguments in general; common sense is a better guide than philosophical reasoning":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A new cancer drug costs $150,000 per patient and adds about a year of life. Who should decide whether people get it?":["Healthcare should be paid for through taxes and free to everyone who needs it.",{"A public health service, for anyone who needs it. Health shouldn't depend on income":"Strongly agree","A public body that weighs its cost against other treatments and funds it only if it is good value":"Agree","Insurers, with the state covering only those who cannot buy cover":"Disagree","Patients and their insurers. The state shouldn't ration care or pay for it":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Water bills have doubled since the water company was privatised, while it paid out large dividends. What should happen?":["Services like water, power and railways are best run by private companies.",{"Bring it back into public ownership. A natural monopoly shouldn't make private profit":"Strongly disagree","Turn it into a non-profit company owned by its customers":"Disagree","Keep it private, but cap prices and dividends through a tougher regulator":"Agree","Open the network to competing suppliers. The problem is monopoly, not private ownership":"Strongly agree"}],
  "The top 1% earn about a fifth of national income. What should the top rate of income tax be?":["People on the highest incomes should pay a much higher rate of tax than everyone else.",{"70% or more, as in the post-war decades. Incomes that extreme shouldn't exist":"Strongly agree","Around 50%: enough to fund services without driving high earners away":"Agree","Around 35%. Higher rates raise little, because the rich move or hide their income":"Disagree","One flat rate for everyone. Taxing success more heavily is unfair":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should there be an annual tax on very large fortunes, say 2% a year on wealth above $50 million?":["An annual tax on people's wealth, on top of the tax on their income, is unfair.",{"Yes, and higher. Concentrated wealth becomes concentrated political power":"Strongly disagree","Yes, if it can be collected without the money simply leaving the country":"Disagree","No. Tax income and inheritance instead, which is simpler and fairer":"Agree","No. That wealth was taxed when it was earned, and this punishes saving and investment":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Raising the minimum wage by a quarter would lift millions of incomes, but economists disagree on how many jobs it would cost. What would you do?":["The minimum wage should be high enough to live on, even if some jobs are lost as a result.",{"Raise it to a living wage by law. A firm that can't pay a decent wage shouldn't exist":"Strongly agree","Raise it step by step, with an independent commission watching the job numbers":"Agree","Keep it low and top up low pay through tax credits instead":"Disagree","Abolish it. A low-paid job beats no job, and pay should be agreed freely":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Rail workers strike for a 10% pay rise, shutting down commuter trains for a week. Where do you stand?":["Workers in essential services, such as hospitals and public transport, should not be allowed to strike.",{"With the strikers. Withdrawing labour is the only real power workers have":"Strongly disagree","They have the right, but essential services should keep a minimum service running":"Disagree","Strikes in essential services should need a high ballot turnout and long notice":"Agree","Essential workers shouldn't be allowed to strike. The public can't be held hostage":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A man has been on unemployment benefits for two years and has turned down three job offers. What should happen?":["People who turn down reasonable job offers should lose their unemployment benefits.",{"Keep paying. Support is a right, and forcing people into bad jobs helps no one":"Strongly disagree","Keep paying, but require training or community work in return":"Disagree","Cut his benefits. Support has to depend on taking reasonable work":"Agree","Stop paying. Benefits should only ever be short-term; after that, family and charity should help":"Strongly agree","Benefits should be short and temporary for everyone. Long-term support belongs to family and charity":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Automation could wipe out millions of jobs. Should every adult get a monthly income from the state, with no conditions?":["Every adult should receive a regular income from the state, with no conditions attached.",{"Yes, enough to live on, paid for by taxing the gains from automation":"Strongly agree","A small one, as a floor that lets people retrain or take risks":"Agree","No. Target help at the people who actually lose their jobs":"Disagree","No. It would cost a fortune and erode the habit of work":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Young people in your city spend half their income on rent. Which policy would you back?":["The best way to make housing affordable is to let private developers build far more homes, with fewer planning rules.",{"Rent controls and a huge programme of state-built housing":"Strongly disagree","State-built housing, but no rent controls":"Disagree","Scrap most planning rules so private builders can build far more":"Agree","Leave it to the market. High rents will draw in new building on their own":"Strongly agree"}],
  "One company handles most of the world's online searches and much of its advertising. What should be done?":["The government should break up companies that dominate their whole market.",{"Break it up, as America broke up Standard Oil":"Strongly agree","Regulate it like a utility, with rules on its fees and its use of data":"Agree","Fine it when it abuses its position, case by case":"Disagree","Leave it alone. It won by being better, and rivals can win the same way":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A steelworks employing 4,000 people in a struggling town will close unless the state pays $300 million to keep it open. What should the state do?":["The government should let failing companies close rather than spend public money keeping them open.",{"Pay, and take an ownership stake. The town's survival matters more than the market's verdict":"Strongly disagree","Let it close, but spend the money retraining the workers and reviving the town":"Disagree","Offer a loan, but only if private investors put money in too":"Agree","Let it close. Propping up loss-making firms wastes money and delays change":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Someone inherits $10 million from their parents. How much of it should be taxed?":["Large inheritances should be taxed heavily.",{"Most of it. Inherited fortunes create a class that never has to work":"Strongly agree","A substantial share, perhaps 40%, above a generous threshold":"Agree","A small share, so family businesses and farms aren't broken up":"Disagree","None. Parents have every right to pass on what they built":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "In Germany, workers at large firms elect half of the company's supervisory board. Should your country do the same?":["Workers should elect some of the members of the boards that run large companies.",{"Yes, half the board. Workers have as much at stake as shareholders":"Strongly agree","Yes, a few seats, so their voice is heard":"Agree","Only where the owners choose to allow it":"Disagree","No. Those who risk their capital should control the company":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Graduates earn far more over their lives than non-graduates. Who should pay for their degrees?":["Students, not taxpayers, should pay for their own university education.",{"Everyone, through taxes. Education is a public good":"Strongly disagree","Mostly the state, with modest fees":"Disagree","Graduates, through loans repaid only once they earn enough":"Agree","Students and their families, with universities competing on price":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should the state decide which industries the country builds, such as chips or batteries, and steer money into them?":["The government should choose which industries to build up and invest public money in them.",{"Yes. Planned investment built the economies of South Korea and China":"Strongly agree","Yes, for a few strategic industries, through a public investment bank":"Agree","Only by funding research. Let markets pick the winners":"Disagree","No. Politicians are bad at picking winners and good at subsidising their friends":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Two children grow up in the same city, one to rich parents and one to poor. Thirty years later the first is wealthy and the second is not. What mostly explains it?":["Whether someone ends up rich or poor depends mostly on their own effort and choices.",{"Their own choices and effort. Background matters less than people claim":"Strongly agree","Mostly effort, though the rich child had a head start":"Agree","Mostly the head start: schools, contacts and money":"Disagree","An economy that keeps wealth with the people who already have it":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Government debt has passed 100% of national income. What should be done?":["Reducing government debt should come before protecting public spending.",{"Cut spending hard, popular programmes included. Debt is a bill we leave our children":"Strongly agree","Freeze spending and let growth shrink the debt over time":"Agree","Raise taxes on high earners and big companies to protect public services":"Disagree","Keep borrowing to invest. A country that issues its own currency can afford it":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Delivery riders choose their own hours but get no sick pay or pension. Should the law treat them as employees?":["Gig workers, such as delivery riders, should stay self-employed rather than be given employee rights.",{"Yes. Flexibility shouldn't mean giving up basic rights":"Strongly disagree","Create a middle status with sick pay and a minimum wage, keeping the flexibility":"Disagree","Only if the rider and the platform both agree to it":"Agree","No. Many riders choose this work precisely because it isn't a job":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A war cuts supply and energy prices triple. What should the government do?":["When energy prices soar, the government should cap them by law.",{"Cap prices by law, and make energy firms pay through a windfall tax":"Strongly agree","Give cash to poorer households and leave prices alone":"Agree","Cut energy taxes for a while and fast-track more domestic supply":"Disagree","Nothing. High prices are what make people use less and firms produce more":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Large oil reserves are found off your coast. Who should own them?":["Natural resources such as oil and gas should be owned by the state, with the profits going to the public.",{"A state company, with the profits saved in a national fund, as in Norway":"Strongly agree","The state, licensing private firms to extract them for a share of the revenue":"Agree","Private firms that win the rights at auction and pay normal taxes":"Disagree","Whoever owns the rights. The state should only collect ordinary taxes":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should parents be able to take the money the state spends on their child's education and spend it at any school they choose, private ones included?":["Parents should be able to spend their child's share of public education money at any school, including private ones.",{"Yes. Competition and choice raise standards for everyone":"Strongly agree","Yes, but only among publicly funded schools":"Agree","No. It drains money from the schools most children depend on":"Disagree","No, and private schools should be abolished. Every child should share the same schools":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "The head of a large company earns 300 times what the average worker there does. What should the law say?":["How much a company pays its bosses is for its owners to decide, not the government.",{"Cap pay at a fixed multiple of the lowest wage in the company":"Strongly disagree","No cap, but publish the ratio and give shareholders a binding vote":"Disagree","Nothing. Pay is for the company's owners to decide":"Agree","Nothing. Top pay reflects what top talent is worth":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Meeting climate targets would raise energy costs and close some industries. What should a government do?":["The most polluting industries should be phased out by fixed dates, even if it costs jobs.",{"Ban the most polluting activities by fixed dates, whatever the short-term cost":"Strongly agree","Tax carbon heavily and spend the money on clean energy and on workers who lose out":"Agree","Fund research and let cheaper clean technology win on its own":"Disagree","Put growth first. One country's cuts make little difference to the climate":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Cheap imported steel is putting the country's last steelworks out of business. What should be done?":["Tariffs on imports are needed to protect the country's own industries.",{"High tariffs. A country that can't make its own steel can't defend itself":"Strongly agree","Targeted tariffs on steel sold below its cost":"Agree","Keep trade open and help the workers into new jobs":"Disagree","Welcome it. Cheap steel makes everything built with steel cheaper":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A trade deal would lower prices and boost exports, but expose local farmers to cheaper foreign food. Should your country sign it?":["Protecting local farmers matters more than getting cheaper food from trade deals.",{"Yes. Consumers gain more than farmers lose, and farmers can adapt":"Strongly disagree","Yes, with support to help farmers adjust":"Disagree","Only with farming left out of the deal":"Agree","No. A country should be able to feed itself":"Strongly agree"}],
  "In a pandemic your country couldn't get masks or medicines, because almost all of them were made abroad. What is the lesson?":["Essential goods, such as medicines, should be made in our own country even if that costs more.",{"Bring production of essentials home, even if it costs more":"Strongly agree","Keep stockpiles and some home production for emergencies":"Agree","Buy from many countries instead of one":"Disagree","There isn't one. Global trade coped about as well as anything could":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A foreign state-backed company wants to buy one of your country's leading tech firms. What should happen?":["Foreign companies should be free to buy any business in our country.",{"Block it. Key firms should stay in national hands":"Strongly disagree","Allow it only after a strict security review":"Disagree","Allow it unless the firm works in defence or infrastructure":"Agree","Allow it. Capital should flow freely and the shareholders should decide":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Suppose everyone got richer, but the rich got richer much faster. Would that be a good outcome?":["A wide gap between rich and poor is harmful, even when everyone's living standards are rising.",{"No. A widening gap damages trust, health and democracy even if everyone gains":"Strongly agree","Partly. The growth is good, but taxes should narrow the gap":"Agree","Mostly yes. What matters is whether the poor are better off":"Disagree","Yes. Inequality is what happens when people are free to succeed":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "People live longer and pensions keep getting more expensive. What is the fairest fix?":["People should save for their own retirement rather than depend on a state pension.",{"Raise taxes and keep a generous state pension for everyone":"Strongly disagree","Raise the pension age slowly and keep the state pension":"Disagree","Shrink the state pension and make workplace saving compulsory":"Agree","Let people save for their own retirement and keep what they save":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A new food additive might be harmful, but there is no proof yet. What should regulators do?":["New products should be allowed on sale unless there is evidence that they cause harm.",{"Ban it until it is proven safe":"Strongly disagree","Allow it with warning labels while it is studied":"Disagree","Allow it unless evidence of harm turns up":"Agree","Allow it. Customers and the courts will punish any firm that harms people":"Strongly agree"}],
  "How much of the money you earn is rightly yours?":["Taxes take money that rightly belongs to the people who earned it.",{"All of it. Tax takes what someone else earned":"Strongly agree","Most of it, minus a fair share for public services":"Agree","A good part belongs to the society that made earning it possible":"Disagree","Income is produced together and should be shared out by need":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Police say they could have stopped a terrorist attack that killed 30 people if they could read encrypted messages. Should apps be forced to let them?":["Messaging apps should never have to let the police read people's private messages.",{"Yes. Privacy can't come before preventing mass murder":"Strongly disagree","Yes, with a judge's warrant for each case":"Disagree","No, but police should get more money for traditional methods":"Agree","No. A back door for the police is a back door for hackers and future governments":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Climate protesters block a motorway, and an ambulance is delayed. What should the law do?":["Protests that block roads or stop people getting to work should be banned.",{"Ban disruptive protest and jail repeat offenders":"Strongly agree","Allow protest, but punish blocking roads and emergency services":"Agree","Prosecute only violence and damage":"Disagree","Disruption is the point of protest. The state should put up with it":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Someone posts online that a religious minority are \"parasites\" who should be driven out of the country. Should that be a crime?":["Hateful speech should not be a crime unless it directly calls for violence.",{"Yes. Speech like that leads to violence and should be punished":"Strongly disagree","Only if it clearly calls for violence or targets particular people":"Disagree","No, but platforms should be free to take it down":"Agree","No. The answer to bad speech is more speech, not prison":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A man is convicted of murdering several children, on DNA and video evidence. Should he be executed?":["Murderers who kill several people should face the death penalty.",{"Yes. Some crimes deserve death":"Strongly agree","Only in cases this certain":"Agree","No. Even if this case is certain, others won't be, and mistakes can't be undone":"Disagree","No. The state should never kill a prisoner":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Portugal decriminalised possession of all drugs in 2001. Should your country do the same?":["People caught with drugs for their own use should be prosecuted.",{"Go further: legalise and regulate all drugs, like alcohol":"Strongly disagree","Yes, treat drug use as a health problem, and legalise cannabis":"Disagree","Decriminalise cannabis only":"Agree","No. Drugs wreck lives, and the law should say so":"Strongly agree"}],
  "After a mass shooting, many people demand stricter gun laws. Where do you stand?":["Law-abiding adults should have the right to own a gun.",{"Citizens have a right to own and carry guns. Disarming the law-abiding doesn't stop criminals":"Strongly agree","Allow guns, with background checks and licences":"Agree","Only hunting and sporting guns, under strict control":"Disagree","Ban private guns. No right outweighs the lives lost":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A president cut crime and corruption dramatically, but did it by ignoring the courts and jailing critics. Was it worth it?":["A leader should never ignore the courts, even to fight crime or corruption.",{"Yes. Results matter more than procedures":"Strongly disagree","In a real crisis, for a limited time":"Disagree","No. The gains won't last once power is unchecked":"Agree","No. Giving up the rule of law is never worth it":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Your side narrowly loses an election, and the winners plan policies you believe will do serious harm. What should your side do?":["When your side loses an election, you should accept the result, even if the winners' plans seem harmful.",{"Accept it and fight them in parliament and at the next election":"Strongly agree","Accept it, but challenge any real irregularities in court":"Agree","Refuse to recognise the result until every doubt is investigated":"Disagree","Block them by any means. Preventing the harm matters more than one vote":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should big constitutional decisions, like leaving a union of countries, be settled by a public vote?":["Big decisions, such as leaving an international union, should be made by parliament rather than by a public vote.",{"Yes, always. The people are sovereign":"Strongly disagree","Yes, but only with a high bar, such as 60%":"Disagree","No. Elected parliaments should decide after proper debate":"Agree","No. Such decisions are too complex for a yes-or-no vote":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should interest rates be set by independent central bankers or by elected politicians?":["Interest rates should be set by independent experts, not by elected politicians.",{"Independent bankers. Politicians would cut rates before every election":"Strongly agree","Bankers, within targets set by parliament":"Agree","Politicians, advised by experts":"Disagree","Politicians. Big economic choices should answer to voters":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A newspaper publishes leaked documents showing the defence minister lied about a war. The government says publication endangers soldiers. What should happen?":["The government should be able to stop newspapers publishing secrets that could harm national security.",{"Nothing to the paper. The public's right to know comes first":"Strongly disagree","Nothing, though the paper should leave out details that could get people killed":"Disagree","A judge should decide whether publication can be stopped":"Agree","Prosecute the paper. National security comes before the press":"Strongly agree"}],
  "How long should the state be able to hold a suspected terrorist without charging them?":["Suspected terrorists should be able to be held for weeks without being charged.",{"As long as intelligence says they are dangerous":"Strongly agree","A few weeks, with a judge's approval":"Agree","A few days, then charge them or let them go":"Disagree","Not at all. No one should be held without charge":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "In a deadly epidemic, should vaccination be required for everyone who can safely have it?":["The state should never force anyone to be vaccinated.",{"Yes, with fines for refusing":"Strongly disagree","Required for health workers and teachers, not for everyone":"Disagree","Not required, but unvaccinated people can be kept out of some places":"Agree","No. The state has no right to inject anyone against their will":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should every 18-year-old have to spend a year in national service?":["Every young adult should spend a year in military service.",{"Yes, in the military. It builds discipline and a ready defence":"Strongly agree","Yes, choosing military or civilian service":"Agree","Only as a voluntary scheme with good incentives":"Disagree","No. The state has no claim on a year of anyone's life":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "In a deadly pandemic, the government orders everyone to stay home for three months. Was that right?":["Ordering everyone to stay at home during a pandemic goes too far, however serious the disease.",{"Yes, and it should be strictly enforced. Lives come first":"Strongly disagree","Yes, if it is short, reviewed often and approved by parliament":"Disagree","It should have been advice. Adults can weigh the risks themselves":"Agree","No. Locking down a whole free society is never justified":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Your country is short of workers, but many citizens say immigration is too high. What would you do?":["Immigration should be cut sharply, even if that leaves jobs unfilled.",{"Open the borders much wider. People should be free to move to where the work is":"Strongly disagree","Keep immigration high, but choose people by skill and need":"Disagree","Cut the numbers and train citizens to fill the gaps":"Agree","Cut immigration sharply. A cohesive nation matters more than filling jobs":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A war next door sends a million refugees toward your border. What should your country do?":["Rich countries have a duty to take in refugees fleeing war.",{"Take in as many as need safety. It's a basic duty":"Strongly agree","Take a fair share, agreed with other countries":"Agree","Pay for camps in neighbouring countries instead of taking them in":"Disagree","Close the border. Our duty is to our own citizens":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "An international court rules that your country's new border policy breaks international law. What should your government do?":["Our parliament should be able to overrule the decisions of international courts.",{"Obey. International law has to bind everyone, us included":"Strongly disagree","Obey, then argue for changing the treaty":"Disagree","Obey only if the ruling fits our own constitution":"Agree","Ignore it. No foreign court stands above our parliament":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should bodies like the UN have the power to enforce decisions on member countries?":["International bodies like the UN should have the power to enforce their decisions on countries.",{"Yes. Global problems need a global authority":"Strongly agree","In a few areas, such as climate and genocide":"Agree","No. They should advise and coordinate, not command":"Disagree","No, and we should leave any that limit our sovereignty":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "In the long run, should humanity aim for a single world government?":["Humanity should eventually unite under a single world government.",{"Yes. It's the only way to end war and solve global problems":"Strongly agree","Perhaps, built slowly from today's institutions":"Agree","No. Cooperation between sovereign nations is enough":"Disagree","No. A bad world government would leave nowhere to escape to":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Your country could gain a big trade advantage, but only by hurting a poorer country's economy. What should it do?":["A government's duty is to its own citizens, even when that means harming people in other countries.",{"Take it. A government's duty is to its own people":"Strongly agree","Take it, and give the poorer country some help":"Agree","Pass it up unless the harm is small":"Disagree","Pass it up. Prosperity built on others' harm is wrong":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Rich countries caused most past emissions; poorer countries now cause most new ones. Who should pay to cut them?":["Each country should pay for its own cuts in emissions, without paying for other countries' cuts.",{"Rich countries, including large payments to poorer ones":"Strongly disagree","Everyone, under a binding treaty that weighs history and wealth":"Disagree","Each country for itself, as it chooses":"Agree","Not my country while bigger emitters keep polluting":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Your country spends 1% of its income on defence. A hostile neighbour spends 5%. What should you do?":["A strong military is the best way to prevent war.",{"Rearm fast to match them. Strength is what prevents war":"Strongly agree","Raise spending steadily and deepen alliances":"Agree","Rely mainly on alliances and diplomacy":"Disagree","Refuse an arms race. Spend the money at home and push for talks":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should a country with nuclear weapons give them up?":["Countries that have nuclear weapons should keep them.",{"Yes, unilaterally. Someone has to go first":"Strongly disagree","Yes, but only in a deal where everyone does":"Disagree","No, not while hostile states still have them":"Agree","No. Nuclear weapons are why the great powers haven't fought each other since 1945":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A neighbouring country seizes a disputed island. What should your government do?":["No disputed piece of territory is worth going to war over.",{"Protest and take it to international courts. No island is worth a war":"Strongly agree","Impose sanctions and build alliances against them":"Agree","Send forces to show any further move will be resisted":"Disagree","Take it back by force. Giving way invites more aggression":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A government is massacring an ethnic minority. Should other countries intervene militarily without UN approval?":["Other countries should use military force to stop a genocide, even without the approval of the UN.",{"Yes. Stopping genocide matters more than legal process":"Strongly agree","Arm the victims and impose sanctions, but send no troops":"Agree","Only with UN approval":"Disagree","No. Foreign interventions usually make things worse":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A region with its own language wants to set its own laws and taxes. Should it be allowed?":["A region should not be allowed to break away from its country, even if its people vote to leave.",{"Yes, on almost everything, and to leave the country if it votes to":"Strongly disagree","Yes, on local matters such as schools and policing":"Disagree","Only minor matters. One country needs one set of laws":"Agree","No. Handing power to regions breaks countries apart":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Who should decide whether a big wind farm or prison is built next to your town?":["Local communities, not the national government, should decide what gets built in their area.",{"The town, by a local vote":"Strongly agree","The town, within national rules":"Agree","The national government, after consulting local people":"Disagree","The national government. Otherwise nothing ever gets built":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A popular president wants to change the constitution so they can run for a third term. Should that be allowed?":["A popular leader should be able to stay in office for as long as voters keep electing them.",{"No. Term limits protect democracy, even from popular leaders":"Strongly disagree","Only through a referendum with a large majority":"Disagree","Yes, if parliament agrees":"Agree","Yes. If the people want them, they should be able to keep them":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A billionaire gives $50 million to a political party. Should that be allowed?":["There should be strict limits on how much money anyone can give to a political party.",{"No. Only small donations, or state funding of parties":"Strongly agree","Yes, but capped at a modest amount":"Agree","Yes, as long as it is made public":"Disagree","Yes. Giving money to a cause is a form of free speech":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A police officer shoots an unarmed man during a chaotic arrest. What should follow?":["Every time a police officer shoots someone, it should be investigated by a body independent of the police.",{"Back the officer. Police make split-second calls and need our support":"Strongly disagree","A review by the police force itself":"Disagree","An independent investigation":"Agree","An independent investigation, and prosecution if the shooting wasn't necessary":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should people be free to live and work in any country they choose?":["People should be free to live and work in any country they choose.",{"Yes. Where you happen to be born shouldn't decide your life":"Strongly agree","Within groups of similar countries, as in the EU":"Agree","Only with a visa and a job offer":"Disagree","No. Each nation decides who joins it":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Riots spread across several cities and the police can't control them. Should the army be deployed?":["The army should never be used against a country's own citizens, even during riots.",{"Yes, immediately":"Strongly disagree","Yes, if the police are overwhelmed for days":"Disagree","Only to guard key buildings, never against crowds":"Agree","No. Soldiers must never be turned on citizens":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should voting be compulsory, as it is in Australia?":["Voting in elections should be compulsory.",{"Yes, with fines for not voting":"Strongly agree","Yes, with a \"none of the above\" option":"Agree","No, but voting should be made much easier":"Disagree","No. Not voting is a choice too":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should same-sex couples be able to marry and adopt on the same terms as everyone else?":["Marriage should only be between a man and a woman.",{"Yes, fully equal in law":"Strongly disagree","Yes, but religious groups shouldn't have to perform the weddings":"Disagree","Civil partnerships with the same rights, but not marriage":"Agree","No. Marriage is between a man and a woman":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A woman is 20 weeks pregnant and wants an abortion for personal reasons. Should it be legal?":["A woman should have the legal right to choose an abortion.",{"Yes. It's her body and her decision, at any stage":"Strongly agree","Yes, up to about 24 weeks, when the foetus could survive outside the womb":"Agree","Only in the first 12 weeks, or later for serious medical reasons":"Disagree","No. Abortion ends a human life, except perhaps to save the mother":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A 15-year-old says they are transgender. What should happen?":["Under-18s should not be given medical treatment to change their gender.",{"Support their identity, including medical treatment if doctors agree":"Strongly disagree","Support a social transition, but delay medical steps until adulthood":"Disagree","Offer counselling first and be cautious about any transition":"Agree","Treat it as something they will likely grow out of. Children can't make this decision":"Strongly agree"}],
  "In a family with young children, who should mainly earn and who should mainly care?":["In a family with young children, the mother should be the main carer and the father the main earner.",{"The father should earn and the mother should care. That is the natural order":"Strongly agree","That usually works best, though each family can choose":"Agree","Whatever suits the couple. Neither role belongs to one sex":"Disagree","Both should share both equally. Anything else holds women back":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Is sex before marriage wrong?":["People should wait until they are married before having sex.",{"Yes. Sex belongs inside marriage":"Strongly agree","It's best kept for a committed relationship":"Agree","It's fine between consenting adults who care for each other":"Disagree","It's nobody's business but the people involved":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A man with motor neurone disease, fully lucid, asks a doctor to help him die. Should that be legal?":["Doctors should never be allowed to help a patient end their own life.",{"Yes. His life, his choice":"Strongly disagree","Yes, with strict safeguards and approval from two doctors and a judge":"Disagree","No. Vulnerable people would come under pressure to choose death":"Agree","No. Life is not ours to end":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A town has held the same festival every year for 400 years. Few people remember why, and it costs a lot. Should it carry on?":["Old customs are worth keeping even when no one can say what they are for.",{"Yes. Customs that last this long hold wisdom we can't always see":"Strongly agree","Usually, unless it clearly harms people":"Agree","Only if people today still get real value from it":"Disagree","No. It should be judged on its merits, not its age":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Compared with 60 years ago, is society better or worse off morally?":["Society today is morally better than it was 60 years ago.",{"Worse. Family, faith and community have collapsed":"Strongly disagree","Worse in some ways, better in others":"Disagree","Mostly better, despite some real losses":"Agree","Far better. We have shed a great deal of cruelty and prejudice":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A religious group wants its teachings to shape national laws on marriage and alcohol. Should they?":["A country's laws should be guided by religious teaching.",{"No. Laws should be secular, whatever the majority believes":"Strongly disagree","Religious people can argue their case, but laws need non-religious reasons":"Disagree","A nation's faith can rightly shape some of its laws":"Agree","Yes. Laws should follow God's commands":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Can a society stay moral without religion?":["A society cannot stay moral without religion.",{"No. Without God, morality has no foundation":"Strongly agree","With difficulty. Religion holds much of it together":"Agree","Yes. Morality can rest on reason and empathy":"Disagree","Yes, and it does better without religion":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should state schools hold daily prayers?":["State schools should not hold religious worship.",{"Yes, in the country's main faith":"Strongly disagree","Yes, with families free to opt out":"Disagree","Teach about all religions, but hold no worship":"Agree","No. State schools should be fully secular":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should religious communities be able to run their own courts for marriage and inheritance disputes, if both sides agree?":["Religious communities should be allowed their own courts for family disputes, as long as both sides agree to use them.",{"Yes. Communities should be able to live by their own law":"Strongly agree","Yes, as long as anyone can still go to a civil court":"Agree","Only as mediation, with no legal force":"Disagree","No. One law for everyone":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "What should a country expect of newcomers?":["Immigrants should adopt the culture of their new country and leave old customs behind.",{"To adopt its culture fully and leave old customs behind":"Strongly agree","To learn the language and accept its core values":"Agree","To obey the law. Their culture is their own business":"Disagree","Nothing special. Diversity enriches a country":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Is a country stronger when many cultures keep their own traditions?":["A country needs one shared culture to hold it together.",{"Yes. Diversity brings new ideas and energy":"Strongly disagree","Yes, if there is a strong shared civic identity":"Disagree","Only up to a point. Too much diversity erodes trust":"Agree","No. A nation needs one shared culture":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should teachers in state schools be allowed to wear religious dress, such as a headscarf or a cross?":["Teachers in state schools should not wear religious symbols or dress at work.",{"No. State institutions must be religiously neutral":"Strongly agree","Small symbols yes, face coverings no":"Agree","Yes, unless it gets in the way of the job":"Disagree","Yes. People should wear what their faith asks of them":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should public services be offered in immigrants' own languages?":["Public services should be offered only in the national language.",{"Yes. Nobody should lose access to services because of language":"Strongly disagree","For essentials such as health and the courts":"Disagree","Only for the first few years after arrival":"Agree","No. Everything in the national language encourages people to learn it":"Strongly agree"}],
  "You're offered your dream job abroad, but your elderly parents and community depend on you. What do you do?":["Your duty to your family should come before your own ambitions.",{"Stay. My duty to them comes first":"Strongly agree","Stay, unless I can arrange good care for them":"Agree","Go, and help them from a distance":"Disagree","Go. Nobody should give up their life for others' expectations":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Who should mainly care for elderly parents who can no longer live alone?":["Adult children have no special duty to look after their elderly parents.",{"Their children, at home. It's a family duty":"Strongly disagree","Their children, with help from the state":"Disagree","Professional carers, paid for partly by the family":"Agree","The state and professionals. Adult children owe no such duty":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Which matters more to teach a child: obedience or independence?":["Obedience is one of the most important things a child can learn.",{"Obedience. Independence comes later":"Strongly agree","Mostly obedience, with room to ask why":"Agree","Mostly independence, within respect":"Disagree","Independence. Children should learn to question everything":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Birth rates have fallen far below replacement level. Is having children a duty?":["Whether to have children is a purely personal choice; no one owes it to their family or country.",{"Yes, to your family and your nation":"Strongly disagree","It's the natural path, and the state should encourage it":"Disagree","It's a personal choice, and the state should stay out of it":"Agree","No. Fewer people is no bad thing":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Who is responsible for whether your life goes well?":["How your life turns out is mainly your own responsibility.",{"Mainly me":"Strongly agree","Mostly me, with help from family and friends":"Agree","Me and my community together":"Disagree","Society. A fair society produces good lives":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should parents be able to edit their embryos' genes, to prevent disease or to make their children taller or smarter?":["Changing the genes of unborn children should be banned.",{"Both. Giving your children better genes is good parenting":"Strongly disagree","To prevent disease yes, and enhancement once it is proven safe":"Disagree","Only to prevent serious disease":"Agree","Neither. We shouldn't redesign human beings":"Strongly agree"}],
  "An AI diagnoses cancer more accurately than doctors. Should it make the diagnosis on its own?":["If an AI is more accurate than doctors, it should be allowed to diagnose patients on its own.",{"Yes. Accuracy saves lives":"Strongly agree","Yes, with a doctor checking its work":"Agree","It should advise, but a doctor must decide":"Disagree","No. Decisions like this must stay human":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should people be free to put implants in their brains to improve memory and intelligence?":["Brain implants that boost memory or intelligence should be banned.",{"Yes. It is the next step for humanity":"Strongly disagree","Yes, once it is proven safe":"Disagree","Only to treat illness or disability":"Agree","No. It would change what it means to be human":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should research on AI that could surpass human intelligence be paused?":["Research into AI that could become more intelligent than humans should be paused.",{"Yes, worldwide. Some doors shouldn't be opened":"Strongly agree","Slowed down and tightly regulated":"Agree","Regulated, but not slowed":"Disagree","No. Progress shouldn't be stopped, and rivals won't stop anyway":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Is it wrong to raise and kill animals for food when we could live without meat?":["There is nothing wrong with raising and killing animals for food.",{"Yes. Their suffering counts for more than our taste":"Strongly disagree","Factory farming is, but humane farming is acceptable":"Disagree","No, though cruelty should be banned":"Agree","No. Animals are here for our use":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should social media users have to verify their real identity?":["People should be free to use social media without revealing their real identity.",{"No. Anonymity protects dissidents and vulnerable people":"Strongly agree","No, but platforms should verify users privately to deal with abuse":"Agree","Yes, for accounts with large audiences":"Disagree","Yes, for everyone. Real names curb abuse":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A transgender woman wants to compete in elite women's sport. Should she be allowed?":["Transgender women should be allowed to compete in women's sport.",{"No. Women's sport should be based on sex at birth":"Strongly disagree","No, but there should be an open category anyone can enter":"Disagree","Yes, if her testosterone has been suppressed for years":"Agree","Yes. She is a woman and should compete as one":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Your $1,000 could pay for a holiday, or save a child's life abroad through a reliable charity. What do you owe that child?":["If you can afford it, you have a duty to give money that would save the lives of strangers in poor countries.",{"The money. Distance doesn't change what a life is worth":"Strongly agree","Some of it. I should give regularly":"Agree","Something, but my own family and country come first":"Disagree","Nothing. Charity is a choice, not a duty":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A rich man and a poor man commit the same crime. Should they get exactly the same sentence?":["A poor person should get a lighter sentence than a rich person who committed the same crime.",{"Yes, exactly. Equality before the law is everything":"Strongly disagree","Yes, but fines should be scaled to income":"Disagree","Mostly, though circumstances should count":"Agree","No. Poverty and background should weigh heavily":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Some children inherit fortunes while others inherit nothing. Is that unjust?":["It is unfair that some children inherit fortunes while others inherit nothing.",{"Yes, deeply":"Strongly agree","Somewhat. It should be balanced out":"Agree","Not really. Parents are entitled to help their children":"Disagree","No. Life was never meant to be equal":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Your brother has committed a serious crime. Do you report him?":["If a member of my family committed a serious crime, I would report them to the police.",{"No. Family comes before the law":"Strongly disagree","No, but I'd push him to turn himself in":"Disagree","Yes, if others are at risk":"Agree","Yes. The law applies to everyone, family included":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A soldier leaks evidence that his own army committed war crimes. Is he a traitor?":["A soldier who leaks evidence of his own army's war crimes has betrayed his country.",{"Yes. You don't betray your own side in wartime":"Strongly agree","He should have raised it inside the army first":"Agree","No, but he must face trial and explain himself":"Disagree","No. He is a hero for exposing the truth":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should children be taught to respect teachers and elders, even when they disagree with them?":["Children should be taught to question their teachers and parents.",{"Yes, without question":"Strongly disagree","Yes, and disagree politely":"Disagree","Respect has to be earned":"Agree","No. They should learn to challenge authority":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Your manager orders you to do something you think is wrong but isn't illegal. What do you do?":["If your employer tells you to do something legal that you think is wrong, you should still do it.",{"Do it. The responsibility lies with the person giving the order":"Strongly agree","Do it, and object through proper channels":"Agree","Refuse unless they can justify it":"Disagree","Refuse. My conscience comes first":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "In private, two adults freely do something most people find disgusting, such as eating a pet that died naturally. No one is harmed. Is it wrong?":["If an act harms no one, it cannot be morally wrong, however disgusting it seems.",{"Yes. Some acts degrade people even when no one is harmed":"Strongly disagree","Sometimes":"Disagree","Rarely. Harm is what matters":"Agree","No. If no one is harmed, it isn't wrong":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Is your body something sacred that you shouldn't degrade, or yours to do whatever you like with?":["The human body is sacred, and should not be degraded, even by its owner.",{"Sacred. It is a trust, not a possession":"Strongly agree","Mostly sacred. Some things really do debase a person":"Agree","That is up to each person":"Disagree","It is mine to do whatever I like with":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Someone kicks a stray dog for fun. How seriously should that be taken?":["Cruelty to animals should be punished as a serious crime.",{"Very. Cruelty to the defenceless is among the worst things a person can do":"Strongly agree","Seriously. It should be a crime":"Agree","It's wrong, but not a matter for the law":"Disagree","Not very. Animals aren't people":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Online pornography is available to anyone with a phone. What should the law do?":["Adults should be free to watch pornography.",{"Ban it. It corrupts people and society":"Strongly disagree","Restrict it heavily, with strict age checks":"Disagree","Allow it for adults, with age checks":"Agree","Leave it alone. Adults decide for themselves":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A couple with young children have grown unhappy, though not abusive. Should they divorce?":["Parents of young children should stay married unless there is abuse.",{"No. Marriage is for life, especially with children":"Strongly agree","Only as a last resort, after years of trying":"Agree","If they've really tried and it isn't working":"Disagree","Yes, if they want to. Staying unhappy helps no one":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A runaway train will kill five people unless you pull a lever that sends it onto a track where it will kill one person. What do you do?":["It would be right to divert a runaway train onto a track where it will kill one person, if that saves five others.",{"Pull it. Five deaths are worse than one, so saving five is right":"Strongly agree","Pull it, because saving more lives matters most, though I'd feel terrible about the one":"Agree","Leave it. The train killing five is a tragedy, but steering it into someone would be killing":"Disagree","There's no correct choice here. Whatever feels right to each person is right for them":"Strongly disagree","Pull the lever. Saving five is clearly better than saving one":"Strongly agree","Pull it, but I would feel guilty whichever way I went":"Agree","Don't pull it. I shouldn't actively cause someone's death":"Disagree","There is no right answer. It just comes down to how people feel":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Your closest friend is about to put their life savings into a business you're sure will fail. They ask what you honestly think. Do you tell them?":["Some moral rules must never be broken, whatever the consequences.",{"Yes, bluntly. They asked for the truth, and honesty is what I owe them":"Strongly agree","Yes, but kindly, and then help them improve the plan":"Agree","Only if hearing it would actually change their mind; otherwise it just hurts them":"Disagree","No. Crushing their dream does more harm than good, and I could be wrong anyway":"Strongly disagree","Yes, bluntly. Their savings matter more than their feelings":"Strongly agree","Yes, gently, and help them fix the plan":"Agree","Only if they seem ready to hear it":"Disagree","No. It is their dream, and my doubts could be wrong":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "You find a wallet with $2,000 and an ID card. No one saw you, and you're behind on rent. What do you do?":["If you could do wrong and never be caught, there would be no good reason left to act fairly.",{"Return all of it. Keeping it would be stealing, whether or not anyone knows":"Strongly disagree","Return all of it, because an honest person does that even when it's hard":"Disagree","Return it, mainly because I'd want someone to do the same for me":"Agree","Keep the cash and post the cards back. I need the money more than they probably do":"Strongly agree","Return it. It's the right thing whether or not anyone knows":"Strongly disagree","Return it, mainly because of the kind of person I want to be":"Disagree","Return it, mostly because I would want mine back":"Agree","Keep the cash and post back the cards. I need it more than they probably do":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A well-run scientific study contradicts something you strongly believe. What do you do?":["It is wrong to believe anything without enough evidence for it.",{"Read how the study was done, and change my mind if it holds up":"Strongly agree","Take it seriously, but wait for other studies to confirm it before changing my mind":"Agree","Look hard for what's wrong with it. One study rarely proves anything":"Disagree","Keep my belief. Studies are often overturned, and my own experience counts too":"Strongly disagree","Check the study's methods, and change my mind if they hold up":"Strongly agree","Take it seriously, but wait for others to repeat it before changing my mind":"Agree","Look hard for its flaws. One study rarely settles anything":"Disagree","Keep my view. Studies get overturned, and my own experience is evidence too":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A teleporter scans you, destroys your body, and builds an exact copy on Mars with all your memories. Is the person on Mars you?":["If a machine destroyed your body and built an exact copy with all your memories, the copy would really be you.",{"Yes. Same memories and personality means the same person":"Strongly agree","Probably, as long as every memory and thought arrived intact":"Agree","No. I died, and someone exactly like me was made":"Disagree","It's impossible to say. There is no way to test who it really is":"Strongly disagree","Probably, as long as nothing was lost on the way":"Agree","Nobody can really know":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "How sure can you be about the things you believe are true?":["We can never be certain that the world outside our own minds exists.",{"Very sure. Most of what I believe is plainly true":"Strongly disagree","Fairly sure. I might be wrong on details, but not on most things":"Disagree","Not very. Many things I'm confident about could turn out to be wrong":"Agree","Hardly at all. I can't be certain of almost anything, even what I see":"Strongly agree","Fairly sure about most things":"Disagree","Not very. I could be wrong about a lot":"Agree","Hardly at all. Almost anything I believe could turn out to be false":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Someone says: \"Morality is just opinion, so nothing is truly wrong.\" What's your reaction?":["Some actions are morally wrong whatever any person or culture believes about them.",{"They're wrong. Some things, like torturing a child for fun, are wrong whatever anyone thinks":"Strongly agree","Mostly wrong. Basic right and wrong comes from human nature, though cultures add their own rules":"Agree","Partly right. Most of what we call right and wrong has changed a lot between cultures and eras":"Disagree","They're right. Right and wrong are just rules that people made up":"Strongly disagree","They're wrong. Some things, like torture, are wrong whatever anyone thinks":"Strongly agree","Partly wrong. Morality is rooted in human nature but shaped by culture":"Agree","Partly right. Morality changes a lot between cultures and eras":"Disagree","They're right. Morality is invented by people":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A criminal's brain scan shows his violent urges came from a childhood injury he didn't choose. Should he be punished like anyone else?":["Our choices are not fully decided by earlier causes; we could genuinely have chosen differently.",{"Yes. Whatever caused his urges, he still chose to act on them":"Strongly agree","A bit less, but he is still responsible for what he did":"Agree","Much less. The injury, not him, was mostly in control":"Disagree","No. Nobody truly chooses what they do, so blaming anyone makes no sense":"Strongly disagree","Yes. He still chose to act":"Strongly agree","Somewhat less, but he is still responsible":"Agree","Much less. He was largely not in control":"Disagree","No. Nobody truly chooses what they do, so blame makes no sense":"Strongly disagree","No. Nobody truly chooses their actions, so the idea of free will is incoherent":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Your doctor, a respected expert, recommends a treatment that your gut says is wrong. What do you do?":["If something seems clearly true when we think it over carefully, that is good evidence it is true, even without proof.",{"Follow the doctor. Gut feelings are often wrong, and they know far more than I do":"Strongly disagree","Follow the doctor, but ask questions until I understand why":"Disagree","Get a second opinion before deciding, since experts can be wrong too":"Agree","Go with my gut. I know my own body better than anyone":"Strongly agree","Follow the expert. Gut feelings are unreliable":"Strongly disagree","Follow the expert, but ask a lot of questions":"Disagree","Get a second opinion and weigh both":"Agree","Go with my instinct. I know my own body better than any expert":"Strongly agree"}],
  "In one country eating dog meat is normal; in another it is seen as cruel. Can one side be shown to be right?":["An action can be wrong in one culture and right in another, without either culture being mistaken.",{"Yes. One side is simply right, and careful reasoning can show which":"Strongly disagree","Usually. Most cultures share basic values like avoiding cruelty, so we can settle it from those":"Disagree","Only partly. Some disagreements like this simply have no right answer":"Agree","No. Each culture decides what is right for itself":"Strongly agree","Usually, by appealing to values all humans share":"Disagree","Sometimes, but often there is no single right answer":"Agree"}],
  "When you die and your brain stops, what happens to you?":["The mind can go on existing after the body dies.",{"I carry on in some form, because I am more than my brain":"Strongly agree","I'm not sure I simply end. There may be more to a person than the brain":"Agree","I stop existing. My mind is just what my brain does":"Disagree","Nobody can know what happens, so I don't take a side":"Strongly disagree","I carry on in some form. I am more than my brain":"Strongly agree","I'm not sure, but there may be more to us than the brain":"Agree","Nothing. When the brain stops, I stop":"Disagree","Nobody can know":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A friend holds a strong belief with no evidence, and it makes them happy. Is that okay?":["Believing in God can be reasonable even without any argument or evidence for it.",{"That's fine. Some of the most important things have to be believed on faith":"Strongly agree","That's fine, as long as the belief doesn't hurt anyone":"Agree","It's their right, but believing things without evidence is a mistake":"Disagree","It's not okay. We should only believe what the evidence supports":"Strongly disagree","Yes. Some beliefs have to be taken on faith":"Strongly agree","Yes, as long as it harms nobody":"Agree","Not really, but it's their right":"Disagree","No. Beliefs should follow the evidence":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A painting is praised by art experts, but you find it ugly. Who is right?":["Some things are more beautiful than others, regardless of anyone's personal taste.",{"The experts. Some art really is better than other art, and they have learned to see it":"Strongly agree","Both of us, partly. Some things about art can be judged, but personal taste matters too":"Agree","Neither of us. What counts as beautiful depends on the culture you grew up in":"Disagree","I am, for me. Beauty is just personal taste":"Strongly disagree","The experts. Beauty has real standards":"Strongly agree","Both are partly right":"Agree","Neither. It depends on your culture":"Disagree","I am. Beauty is personal taste":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Looking at the last few centuries, is the world getting better?":["Over the centuries, humanity has made real moral progress.",{"Yes, clearly. People live longer, freer and kinder lives than ever before":"Strongly agree","Mostly yes, though there have been terrible setbacks along the way":"Agree","Only our technology has improved. People are as cruel as they always were":"Disagree","No. Every step forward brings a new problem just as bad":"Strongly disagree","Yes, clearly. Reason and morality have advanced":"Strongly agree","Mostly yes, with serious setbacks":"Agree","Only our tools improve. People are as cruel as ever":"Disagree","No. Every gain brings a new loss":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A man commits a crime, is truly remorseful, and is no danger to anyone. Should he still go to prison?":["Criminals should be punished because they deserve it, even if the punishment prevents no future crime.",{"Yes. Justice means paying for what you did, however sorry you are now":"Strongly agree","Yes, but for less time, because his remorse counts for something":"Agree","Probably not. Prison is for protecting people, and he is no danger":"Disagree","No. Punishment should only be about helping him change and preventing future harm":"Strongly disagree","Yes. Justice means paying for what you did, whatever you feel now":"Strongly agree","Yes, but for a shorter time":"Agree","Probably not. Punishment should prevent future crime":"Disagree","No. The goal should be rehabilitation":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "What are your thoughts and feelings made of?":["The mind is nothing more than the physical activity of the brain.",{"Brain activity, and nothing more":"Strongly agree","Brain activity, though nobody yet understands how it creates feelings":"Agree","Partly the brain, partly something that isn't physical":"Disagree","Mainly a soul or spirit, which the brain only carries":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Which is closest to your view of God?":["There is a God who created the universe.",{"A personal God exists, who knows and cares about each of us":"Strongly agree","Something divine or spiritual exists, but not a God who is a person":"Agree","There is probably no god, but I can't be sure":"Disagree","There are no gods":"Strongly disagree","A personal God exists":"Strongly agree","Something divine exists, but not as a person":"Agree","Probably no god, but I can't be sure":"Disagree","No gods exist":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "How do you decide what is true?":["Everything we know about the world ultimately comes from experience and observation.",{"Mostly by what can be seen, tested and measured":"Strongly agree","Evidence first, checked with careful reasoning":"Agree","Reasoning, gut feeling and what wise people before me taught":"Disagree","Some truths only come through faith or revelation":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "When you decide whether something is right or wrong, what matters most?":["Whether an action is right or wrong depends only on its consequences.",{"Whether it follows the moral rules, whatever happens":"Strongly disagree","Whether a good person would do it":"Disagree","Whether it leads to the best results for everyone":"Agree","Whether people could fairly agree to it":"Strongly agree"}],
  "At 80 you will have an older body, different views and fading memories. What makes that person still you?":["Each of us has a soul or inner self that stays the same throughout our life.",{"The same brain and body, just older":"Strongly disagree","My memories and personality carrying on":"Disagree","A soul or inner self that stays the same":"Agree","Nothing really. There is no fixed 'me', just a life that keeps changing":"Strongly agree"}],
  "How much should we trust scientific theories?":["Our best scientific theories describe the world as it really is, including the parts we cannot observe.",{"They tell us how the world really is":"Strongly agree","They are our best picture so far, and usually close to the truth":"Agree","They are useful tools, but not necessarily the truth":"Disagree","They are one way of seeing the world, shaped by the culture that made them":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A murderer asks where your friend is hiding. Do you lie to them?":["Lying is wrong even when a lie would save someone's life.",{"No. Lying is always wrong, even to save someone":"Strongly agree","I'd refuse to tell them where my friend is, but I wouldn't lie":"Agree","Yes. Saving my friend's life matters far more than telling the truth":"Disagree","Yes, without a second thought. Honesty doesn't matter at all in a case like this":"Strongly disagree","No. Lying is always wrong, whatever the result":"Strongly agree","I would refuse to answer, but not lie":"Agree","Yes. Saving a life matters far more than honesty":"Disagree","Yes, and I would feel no guilt at all":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A surgeon could secretly kill one healthy patient and use his organs to save five dying patients. Should she?":["It would be right for a surgeon to secretly sacrifice one patient to save five others with his organs.",{"Never. Killing an innocent person is wrong, whatever the numbers":"Strongly disagree","No, though it's hard to explain why five deaths are better than one":"Disagree","Yes. Saving five lives is better than saving one":"Agree","There's no right answer. It depends on each person's values":"Strongly agree","Never. You can't use a person like that, whatever the numbers":"Strongly disagree","No, but the numbers make it hard":"Disagree","Yes. Five lives are worth more than one":"Agree","There's no right answer. It depends on your values":"Strongly agree"}],
  "You promised a dying friend to give their savings to one charity, but another would do far more good. What do you do?":["A promise should be kept even when breaking it would do more good.",{"Keep the promise. I gave my word, and that matters more than the extra good":"Strongly agree","Keep it, though I'd feel bad that the money could do more elsewhere":"Agree","Break it and give to the charity that does the most good":"Disagree","Do what I think my friend would have wanted if they had known the facts":"Strongly disagree","Keep the promise. A promise is a promise":"Strongly agree","Keep it, but feel torn":"Agree","Break it and give where it does the most good":"Disagree","Ask what my friend would really have wanted":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A machine could give you a perfectly happy life that feels completely real, though none of it would be. Would you plug in for good?":["A life of perfect happiness inside a simulation would be just as good as a real life.",{"Yes. Being happy is all that matters":"Strongly agree","Yes, as long as I would never know the difference":"Agree","No. I want a real life, even with the pain":"Disagree","No. Truth and real achievements matter more than feeling good":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Could a computer ever truly feel pain or joy, not just act as if it does?":["A computer could one day have real thoughts and feelings, not just imitate them.",{"Yes. Feelings are just what a brain or a machine does":"Strongly agree","Probably one day, though we don't know how feelings arise":"Agree","Probably not. Feelings seem to need more than processing":"Disagree","No. Real feelings need a soul or something beyond matter":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "When you see the colour red or taste chocolate, is that experience just chemicals in your brain?":["Conscious experience, such as what it is like to see red, cannot be fully explained by facts about the brain.",{"Yes. It is nothing more than brain chemistry":"Strongly disagree","Yes, though science can't fully explain it yet":"Disagree","Not entirely. What it feels like seems to be something more than chemistry":"Agree","No. It shows the mind is something beyond the physical brain":"Strongly agree","Yes, completely":"Strongly disagree","Not entirely. Experience seems to be something extra":"Agree","No. It shows the mind isn't just physical":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Over 500 years, every stone of an old church has been replaced. Is it still the same church?":["Some questions about identity, such as whether a fully rebuilt ship is still the same ship, have no true answer.",{"Yes. Its shape, purpose and history make it the same church":"Strongly disagree","Yes, but only because people decide to call it that":"Disagree","No. Once all the original stones are gone, it is a new building":"Agree","There's no single right answer. Both views make sense":"Strongly agree","Yes. It kept its shape, its purpose and its history":"Strongly disagree","Yes, but only because people choose to call it that":"Disagree","No. It is a new building that looks the same":"Agree","There is no single right answer":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Some people claim we are probably living inside a computer simulation. What do you make of that?":["It is quite possible that we are living in a computer simulation.",{"It can't be tested, so it isn't worth believing":"Strongly disagree","Interesting, but I don't believe it":"Disagree","It's possible. I can't rule it out":"Agree","Quite likely, and it changes how I see everything":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Scientists can sometimes see your brain 'decide' to move a moment before you feel you have chosen. What does that tell you?":["Free will is an illusion: the brain decides before we are aware of choosing.",{"Nothing important. I am still the one who makes the choice":"Strongly disagree","Choices have hidden roots in the brain, but I still choose":"Disagree","Most of our choices are made before we know about them":"Agree","That free will is an illusion. Our brains decide, and we only feel that we chose":"Strongly agree","Nothing important. I still make the choice":"Strongly disagree","Choices have hidden roots, but I still choose":"Disagree","Most of our choices are made before we know it":"Agree","Free will is an illusion":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Two drivers are equally reckless and drunk. One hits a child by chance; the other gets home safely. Should the first be blamed more?":["A drunk driver who kills someone deserves more blame than an equally drunk driver who happened to get home safely.",{"Yes. He killed a child, and what actually happens matters morally":"Strongly agree","A bit more, though both were equally reckless":"Agree","No. They did exactly the same thing, so they deserve the same blame":"Disagree","Blaming either makes little sense. Neither chose what made them the way they are":"Strongly disagree","Yes. Results matter morally":"Strongly agree","A little more, but they are equally reckless":"Agree","No. They deserve the same blame":"Disagree","Neither chose to be that way, so blame is mostly pointless":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Should a free society allow groups that want to end freedom for everyone else?":["Free speech should protect even people who want to abolish free speech.",{"Yes. Freedom has to apply to everyone, even them":"Strongly agree","Mostly, until they actually cause harm":"Agree","No. A free society is allowed to protect itself":"Disagree","There's no neutral answer. It depends on who holds power":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Is it wrong to cause animals pain for food or science, if humans gain a lot?":["Making animals suffer is wrong even when it brings big benefits to humans.",{"It's always wrong. Animals have a right not to be made to suffer":"Strongly agree","It's wrong unless the benefit to humans is huge":"Agree","It's fine, as long as the suffering is kept as low as possible":"Disagree","Animals don't count morally the way people do, so it isn't really a question of right and wrong":"Strongly disagree","Always wrong. Animals have rights":"Strongly agree","Wrong unless the benefit is huge":"Agree","Fine if the suffering is kept as small as possible":"Disagree","Animals do not count morally the way people do":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "People once accepted slavery. Were they wrong, or just different from us?":["Slavery was wrong even in times when almost everyone accepted it.",{"They were wrong. Slavery was always wrong, even when everyone accepted it":"Strongly agree","They were wrong, and humanity has slowly learned more of the truth since":"Agree","Wrong by today's standards, but not by the standards of their own time":"Disagree","Neither. Right and wrong are just whatever a society accepts":"Strongly disagree","Wrong. Some moral truths are timeless":"Strongly agree","Wrong, and we have learned more of the truth since":"Agree","Wrong by today's standards, not by theirs":"Disagree","Neither: morals are only what a society accepts":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Would 2 + 2 = 4 still be true if no humans had ever existed?":["Mathematical truths, such as 2 + 2 = 4, would be true even if no one had ever existed.",{"Yes. It is true whether or not anyone exists":"Strongly agree","Yes, though we only know it through human reasoning":"Agree","Not really. Maths is a human tool that happens to work":"Disagree","No. Maths is a human invention, shaped by our language and culture":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Two explanations both fit the facts, but one is much simpler. Which do you trust?":["When two explanations fit the facts equally well, the simpler one is more likely to be true.",{"The simpler one, always":"Strongly agree","The simpler one usually, unless there is good reason not to":"Agree","Whichever fits best with everything else I know, even if it's more complicated":"Disagree","Neither. I'd wait for more evidence before deciding":"Strongly disagree","The simpler one, usually":"Agree","Whichever fits better with everything else I know, even if it's more complicated":"Disagree","Neither, until there is more evidence":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A friend tells you they saw a UFO last night. How much do you believe them?":["Reports of miracles should almost never be believed.",{"Not until there is evidence":"Strongly agree","A little, unless it's easy to check":"Agree","If they seem honest and sober, I give them the benefit of the doubt":"Disagree","I believe them. Demanding proof for everything is its own mistake":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Someone you trust says they have seen a ghost. What do you think?":["Nothing exists beyond the natural world that science studies.",{"They were mistaken or imagining it. Ghosts don't exist":"Strongly agree","There's probably a normal explanation, but I can't be certain":"Agree","It might be real. Science doesn't know everything":"Disagree","I believe it. There is more to the world than what we can measure":"Strongly disagree","They were mistaken or imagined it":"Strongly agree","Probably a natural explanation, but I cannot be certain":"Agree","It may be real; science does not know everything":"Disagree","It is real. There is more to the world than matter":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Why does the universe exist at all?":["The universe exists because something beyond it, such as God, brought it into being.",{"Because God or a higher mind made it":"Strongly agree","There is a deeper purpose behind it, even if I can't name it":"Agree","It just does. There doesn't have to be a reason":"Disagree","We may never be able to answer that, or even ask it properly":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Does life have a purpose?":["Life has a meaning that exists whether or not we choose to give it one.",{"Yes. God or the universe gave each life a purpose":"Strongly agree","Yes. There is a real purpose we can find by thinking about how to live":"Agree","No built-in purpose, but each of us can make our own":"Disagree","No. Life has no purpose, and searching for one is a mistake":"Strongly disagree","Yes, given by a creator or the universe":"Strongly agree","Yes, one we discover by reflecting":"Agree","No, but we can create our own":"Disagree","No, and looking for one is a mistake":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Is dying bad for the person who dies?":["Death is simply the end of a person; nothing of us carries on.",{"Not if something comes after death":"Strongly disagree","Yes, because they lose everything they would have had":"Disagree","Not really. Once you're dead, nothing can harm you":"Agree","No. Death is simply the end, and there is nothing to fear":"Strongly agree"}],
  "You are writing the rules for a new country, but you don't know whether you will be born rich or poor, healthy or sick in it. Which rule matters most?":["A just society should put the worst-off first, even if that means less wealth or happiness overall.",{"Equal rights for everyone, with no exceptions":"Strongly agree","Equal rights first, then protect whoever is worst off":"Agree","Whatever makes the most people happiest overall":"Disagree","Whatever has actually been shown to work in real countries":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A tradition has lasted a thousand years, but you can't see what it is for. Do you keep it?":["Long-standing traditions deserve respect even when we can't see what they are for.",{"Yes. Lasting a thousand years is evidence that it works":"Strongly agree","Yes. There is probably a reason I can't see":"Agree","Only if it proves its worth":"Disagree","No. If nobody can justify it, it should go":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A person from a terrible background commits a crime. How much did their background decide what they did?":["Whatever our circumstances, each of us is fully responsible for what we make of our life.",{"Very little. They chose to do it, like anyone else would":"Strongly agree","Some, but in the end they still made the choice":"Agree","A lot. Someone from that background had few real options":"Disagree","Almost everything. Most people with that background would have done the same":"Strongly disagree","Very little. They chose":"Strongly agree","Some, but they still chose":"Agree","A lot. They had few real options":"Disagree","Almost everything":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A clever argument concludes something that feels deeply wrong. What do you do?":["If an argument seems flawless but leads to a conclusion that seems absurd, we should accept the conclusion.",{"Accept the argument. My feelings can be wrong":"Strongly agree","Look for the flaw, but accept the conclusion if I can't find one":"Agree","Trust my feeling. If the conclusion is that wrong, the argument must be flawed somewhere":"Disagree","Distrust clever arguments in general. Being able to argue for something doesn't make it true":"Strongly disagree","Trust the argument. Feelings can be wrong":"Strongly agree","Look for the flaw, but accept it if I cannot find one":"Agree","Trust my feeling. The argument must be flawed":"Disagree"}],
  "A perfect copy of you is made and the original is painlessly destroyed. Is the copy still you?":["If a machine destroyed your body and built an exact copy with all your memories, the copy would really be you.",{"Yes. The copy has all the same memories and personality":"Strongly agree","Only if it carries on my thoughts without a break":"Agree","No. It is a new person who thinks it's me":"Disagree","There's no fact of the matter":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Nobody can prove they aren't dreaming right now. How much does that bother you?":["We can never be certain that the world outside our own minds exists.",{"Not at all. Doubting everything is a game, not a real problem":"Strongly disagree","A little, but everyday evidence is good enough":"Disagree","It bothers me. I can't be sure of much":"Agree","Deeply. Almost everything I believe could be wrong":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Two cultures disagree about something one sees as normal and the other as terrible. Can reason settle who is right?":["An action can be wrong in one culture and right in another, without either culture being mistaken.",{"Yes. Reason can find the truth":"Strongly disagree","Often, through shared human values and evidence":"Disagree","Sometimes, but some differences have no right answer":"Agree","No. Each culture sets its own standards":"Strongly agree"}],
  "After you die, your brain stops working. What happens to your mind?":["The mind can go on existing after the body dies.",{"It continues. The mind is more than the brain":"Strongly agree","It ends, but the mind was never just neurons":"Agree","It ends. The mind is what the brain does":"Disagree","Nobody can know":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Which is closest to your view of the mind?":["The mind is nothing more than the physical activity of the brain.",{"It is entirely physical: brain activity and nothing more":"Strongly agree","Mostly physical, though consciousness is hard to explain":"Agree","Mind and body are different kinds of thing that affect each other":"Disagree","The mind is non-physical, perhaps a soul":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Where does real knowledge come from?":["Everything we know about the world ultimately comes from experience and observation.",{"Mainly from experience and observation":"Strongly agree","From both: evidence, checked by reason":"Agree","Partly from reasoning, intuition and tradition":"Disagree","Some of it comes from faith or revelation":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "What makes an action right?":["Whether an action is right or wrong depends only on its consequences.",{"It follows moral rules or duties":"Strongly disagree","It comes from good character":"Disagree","It leads to the best results overall":"Agree","People could reasonably agree to it":"Strongly agree"}],
  "What makes you the same person over time?":["Each of us has a soul or inner self that stays the same throughout our life.",{"My body and brain":"Strongly disagree","My memories and personality":"Disagree","A soul or inner self that carries on":"Agree","Nothing fixed: I am a changing stream of experiences":"Strongly agree"}],
  "How should we think about scientific theories?":["Our best scientific theories describe the world as it really is, including the parts we cannot observe.",{"They describe how the world really is":"Strongly agree","They are our best approximations of reality":"Agree","They are useful tools, not necessarily the truth":"Disagree","They are one story among many, shaped by culture":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A healthy visitor in a hospital could be sacrificed to save five dying patients with his organs, and no one would ever know. Is that right?":["It would be right for a surgeon to secretly sacrifice one patient to save five others with his organs.",{"Never. Using a person like that is wrong, whatever the result":"Strongly disagree","No, but the numbers make it troubling":"Disagree","Yes. Five lives outweigh one":"Agree","There is no fact about it; it depends on your values":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A machine can give you a perfect, happy life that feels exactly like reality. Would you plug in for good?":["A life of perfect happiness inside a simulation would be just as good as a real life.",{"Yes. Happiness is all that matters":"Strongly agree","Yes, if I would never know the difference":"Agree","No. I want a real life, even with pain":"Disagree","No. Truth and real achievements matter more than feeling good":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Mary knows every physical fact about colour but has never seen red. When she first sees it, does she learn something new?":["A computer could one day have real thoughts and feelings, not just imitate them.",{"No. It is just more brain activity":"Strongly agree","Yes, but it can be explained physically":"Agree","Yes. Experience is more than physical facts":"Disagree","Yes. This shows the mind is not physical":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Could there be a person who acts exactly like you but has no inner experience at all?":["Conscious experience, such as what it is like to see red, cannot be fully explained by facts about the brain.",{"No. Behaviour and the brain explain everything":"Strongly disagree","Probably not, but it is hard to prove":"Disagree","Possibly, which would mean consciousness is something extra":"Agree","Yes. Consciousness is not just matter":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Every plank of a ship is replaced, one at a time, until none of the original is left. Is it the same ship?":["Some questions about identity, such as whether a fully rebuilt ship is still the same ship, have no true answer.",{"Yes. It keeps its form and its history":"Strongly disagree","Yes, but only because we choose to say so":"Disagree","No. It is a different ship now":"Agree","There is no single right answer":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Some argue we are probably living in a computer simulation. How do you react?":["It is quite possible that we are living in a computer simulation.",{"It's an untestable story, so it isn't worth believing":"Strongly disagree","Interesting, but I do not believe it":"Disagree","Possible. I cannot rule it out":"Agree","Quite likely, and it changes how I see everything":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Studies show brain activity starts before we feel we have decided to move. What does this show?":["Free will is an illusion: the brain decides before we are aware of choosing.",{"Nothing important. We still decide":"Strongly disagree","That decisions have unconscious roots, but we still choose":"Disagree","That our choices are mostly settled before we are aware":"Agree","That free will is an illusion":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should a tolerant society put up with people who want to destroy tolerance?":["Free speech should protect even people who want to abolish free speech.",{"Yes. Principles must apply to everyone":"Strongly agree","Mostly, until they do real harm":"Agree","No. A society may protect itself":"Disagree","There is no neutral answer; it depends on who holds power":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Is mathematics discovered or invented?":["Mathematical truths, such as 2 + 2 = 4, would be true even if no one had ever existed.",{"Discovered. It exists whether or not we do":"Strongly agree","Discovered, through human reasoning":"Agree","Invented as a useful tool":"Disagree","Invented, and shaped by culture and language":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Two explanations fit the facts equally well, but one is simpler. Which do you trust?":["When two explanations fit the facts equally well, the simpler one is more likely to be true.",{"Whichever fits better with everything else I know, even if it is more complicated":"Disagree","The simpler one, usually":"Agree","Neither, until there is more evidence":"Strongly disagree","The simpler one, always":"Strongly agree"}],
  "A stranger says they saw something astonishing. How much do you believe them?":["Reports of miracles should almost never be believed.",{"A little, unless it is easy to check":"Agree","Believe them. Most people are honest, and demanding proof for everything is its own mistake":"Strongly disagree","Not until there is evidence":"Strongly agree","If they seem honest and sober, I give them the benefit of the doubt":"Disagree"}],
  "Why is there something rather than nothing?":["The universe exists because something beyond it, such as God, brought it into being.",{"A creator or higher mind made it":"Strongly agree","There is a deeper purpose I can sense":"Agree","It is just a brute fact, with no reason":"Disagree","The question may not even make sense":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "Is death bad for the person who dies?":["Death is simply the end of a person; nothing of us carries on.",{"Not if there is something beyond it":"Strongly disagree","Yes, mostly because of everything they lose":"Disagree","Not really. Once dead, no one is harmed":"Agree","No. Fearing death makes no sense":"Strongly agree"}],
  "You design a society without knowing which place in it you will get. What rule do you choose?":["A just society should put the worst-off first, even if that means less wealth or happiness overall.",{"Strong equal rights, no matter what":"Strongly agree","Rights first, then protect the worst off":"Agree","Whatever makes society happiest overall":"Disagree","I would not trust any rule I could not test in practice":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A tradition has lasted a thousand years, but you cannot see its reason. Do you keep it?":["Long-standing traditions deserve respect even when we can't see what they are for.",{"Yes. Surviving a thousand years is itself evidence that it works":"Strongly agree","Yes. There is probably a reason I cannot see":"Agree","Only if it proves its worth":"Disagree","No. If nobody can justify it, it should go":"Strongly disagree"}],
  "A custom has lasted for centuries, but no one can say exactly what it is for. Should it be kept?":["Old customs are worth keeping even when no one can say what they are for.",{"Yes. Customs that survive usually hold wisdom we can't see":"Strongly agree","Only if it can still be justified today":"Disagree","No. A custom should be judged on its merits, whatever its age":"Strongly disagree","Usually, unless it clearly harms people":"Agree"}],
  "Two adults freely agree to do something most people find disgusting, but it harms no one. Is it wrong?":["If an act harms no one, it cannot be morally wrong, however disgusting it seems.",{"Yes. Some acts degrade people even when no one is harmed":"Strongly disagree","Sometimes":"Disagree","Rarely. Harm is what matters":"Agree","No. If no one is harmed, it isn't wrong":"Strongly agree"}],
  "Should the body be treated as something sacred, kept free of degrading things?":["The human body is sacred, and should not be degraded, even by its owner.",{"Yes. The body is a trust, not a possession":"Strongly agree","Mostly. Some things really do debase a person":"Agree","That's up to each person":"Disagree","No. It's ours to do with as we like":"Strongly disagree"}]};
